.TH DANBY-ORBITAL 1
.SH NAME
danby-orbital \- what a hydrogen wave function looks like in space
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B danby/orbital
.br
.B danby/orbital -c
.RB [ -n
.IR n ]
.RB [ -l
.IR l ]
.RB [ -m
.IR m ]
.SH MODEL
.IR Danby-hydrogen (1)
shoots the RADIAL equation and draws u(r): how the electron is
distributed with distance, and nothing at all about direction.  That is
half the answer.
.PP
The other half is the angular part, and it is the half with the shapes
in it \(em the s sphere, the p dumbbells, the d cloverleaves.  Those are
the pictures everyone has seen, and they are the reason chemistry has
directions in it: a bond points somewhere because an orbital does.
.SH DESCRIPTION
Two views of the same state, because neither is enough on its own.
.SS Left: the wave function in three dimensions
Every place where |psi| rises above a chosen fraction of its largest
value, drawn as a point and projected onto the screen, turning slowly.
Colour is the SIGN of psi, not its size, so the lobes are distinguished
by the thing that makes two of them cancel when they overlap.
.PP
That view shows the shape and cannot show the inside, because a
projection is a sum over everything behind everything else.  Which is
exactly what makes it a projection, and exactly what you get when you
look at any solid thing.
.SS Right: a slice
Psi in the y = 0 plane, with sign, as a signed density.  This shows what
the projection hides: the radial nodes, the spheres inside spheres, and
where psi passes through zero rather than merely getting small.
.PP
The pair is the point of the program.  A 2s orbital and a 1s orbital
project to almost the same blob and are not remotely the same thing;
the slice shows the shell inside the shell at once, and the change of
sign across it.
.SS Keys
.B n
and
.B N
step the principal quantum number up and down;
.B l
and
.BR L ,
.B m
and
.B M
the others.  A step that would leave a combination that is not a state
is refused rather than silently corrected, so the numbers on screen are
always a state.
.PP
.B +
and
.B \-
move the contour the cloud sits on \(em down to see the faint outer
parts, up to see only the core.
The arrow keys turn it, space stops the turning, q quits.
.SS Checking it without a display
.B -c
builds the cloud, reports how many points it holds and how their signs
fall about the axes, and exits without opening anything.  The lobes and
their signs are the whole content of the picture, so that is how the
picture is checked where there is no display:
.PP
.EX
danby/orbital -c -n 2 -l 1 -m 0
.EE
.PP
reports 492 positive points above z = 0 and 492 negative below, with
x > 0 holding 246 of each, which is a dumbbell along z and could not be
anything else.
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/danby/orbital.b
.br
.B /appl/lib/orbital.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR danby (1),
.IR danby-hydrogen (1),
.IR orbital (2),
.IR orbitaltest (1)
.SH BUGS
The cloud is a contour and not a density.  Every point inside it is
drawn the same, so where the electron is MOST likely to be does not
stand out from where it is merely likely enough.  The slice carries that
information and the cloud does not.
.PP
Points are drawn far side first so the near side covers them, which is
all that makes a cloud read as solid.  It is not a depth buffer: two
points at nearly the same depth are drawn in grid order.
.PP
The sign convention is the usual one for real harmonics, with p_x's
positive lobe at positive x.  Psi and \-psi are the same state, so the
colours could be swapped throughout without anything being wrong; what
matters is that neighbouring lobes differ, and they do.
.PP
n is capped at 5.  Beyond that the cloud needs a finer grid than it has
to resolve the shells, and would draw a misleading picture rather than a
slow one.
